  "notes": "USAGE:\nThe transitive counterpart of {滅|ほろ}びる (to perish, to be ruined). Carries a dramatic, weighty tone and is often used in historical, literary, or philosophical contexts.\n\nThe proverb {驕|おご}る{者|もの}は{久|ひさ}しからず ({身|み}を{滅|ほろ}ぼす) — \"the arrogant do not last long\" — captures the common figurative usage about self-destruction through excess.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {国|くに}を{滅|ほろ}ぼす: to destroy a nation\n- {身|み}を{滅|ほろ}ぼす: to ruin oneself\n- {敵|てき}を{滅|ほろ}ぼす: to annihilate the enemy\n- {文明|ぶんめい}を{滅|ほろ}ぼす: to destroy a civilization",
